{
    "GHSA-3g3m-2fjr-6pw4": {
        "notes": "CVE-2026-118773: glob's walker calls its callback on an aborted AbortSignal without returning, so an aborted async glob()/globStream() keeps reading directories in the background after it rejects (glob >=9.0.0 <=14.0.1). Only callers that pass a `signal` option are affected. The shipped copy is fixed: the direct dependency is glob ^14.0.3, bundled into dist/extension.node.js through src/platform/common/platform/fileSystem.node.ts (14.0.3 rather than 14.0.2, whose inlined minimatch predates the GHSA-g3hv-75wg-m249 fix). Accepted risk for the remaining copies, all dev-only and not shipped (node_modules/** is excluded from the VSIX): glob 10.5.0 under mocha 11.8.0 and under rimraf 5.0.10 (a root devDependency nothing references), and glob 13.0.6 under @vscode/test-cli 0.0.15, @vscode/vsce 3.9.2, c8 11.0.0 and vscode-extension-tester 8.28.1; in src/test/vscode-notebook-perf (mirrored in its osv-scanner.toml), glob 13.0.6 (hoisted; required by its own devDependency, mocha 12.0.3, @vscode/test-cli and test-exclude 8.0.0) and 10.5.0 under @vscode/test-cli's mocha. None of these tools passes a signal to glob, and nothing runs rimraf, the only one that forwards one. No release of these parents admits the fix: the latest mocha 12.0.3 and rimraf 6.1.3 declare glob ^13, @vscode/test-cli 0.0.15, test-exclude 8.0.0 (c8) and vscode-extension-tester 8.28.1 declare ^13.0.6, and @vscode/vsce drops glob only in the 4.x major. Alternative remediation, verified under npm 10.9.4: the overrides `\"glob@>=9.0.0 <14.0.2\": \"14.0.2\"`, `\"test-exclude@8\": { \"glob\": \"14.0.2\" }` and `\"mocha\": { \"glob\": \"14.0.2\" }` put every copy on 14.x. The value must differ from the root's locked glob version (set to 14.0.3, npm 10.9.4 leaves every copy unpatched with invalid edges), and 14.0.2's inlined minimatch still lacks the GHSA-g3hv-75wg-m249 fix, which scanners cannot see. Not taken because it forces a glob major under the test, coverage and packaging tools for a code path none of them uses.",
        "expiry": "2026-11-15"
    },
    "GHSA-848j-6mx2-7j84": {
        "notes": "CVE-2025-14505: elliptic's ECDSA signing mis-computes the byte length of the nonce k when k has leading zeros and truncates it, emitting an incorrect signature; an attacker who obtains both a faulty and a correct signature over the same input can, under certain conditions, recover the private key. Only ECDSA signing (crypto-browserify's createSign with an EC key) is affected. Accepted risk: dev-only transitive dependency, absent from the production tree (`npm ls elliptic --omit=dev` is empty). Reached only via node-stdlib-browser@1.3.1 -> crypto-browserify@3.12.1 -> browserify-sign@4.2.5 / create-ecdh@4.0.4 -> elliptic@6.6.1. node-stdlib-browser is a devDependency used exclusively by build/esbuild/build.ts, whose stdlib polyfill plugin is applied only to the web test entry (src/test/web/index.ts -> out/extension.web.bundle.js, excluded from the VSIX by `out/**` in .vscodeignore). That bundle does include elliptic, because the plugin resolves node:crypto to crypto-browserify both for @deepnote/convert's static import of createHash and randomUUID, which loads it at startup, and for the Node-only dynamic import in src/platform/common/crypto.ts. The production desktop and web bundles never pull it in, confirmed by the absence of node_modules/elliptic, node_modules/browserify-sign and node_modules/crypto-browserify inputs in dist/*.esbuild.meta.json. No code in this extension or in @deepnote/convert calls createSign, createVerify or createECDH. No patched upstream release is currently available: every published elliptic release is affected (range <=6.6.1, and 6.6.1 is the latest version on npm, published 2024-11-13), GitHub lists no patched version, npm audit reports fixAvailable:false, and the upstream fix PRs (indutny/elliptic#345, #351, #354) are unmerged. Native browser crypto cannot replace the polyfill, because @deepnote/convert needs a synchronous createHash. Alternative remediation if no upstream fix lands: a root `elliptic` override to npm:empty-pkg@1.0.0 plus an alias of elliptic to a `module.exports = {}` stub in the web test build only (browserify-sign and create-ecdh require('elliptic') and empty-pkg ships no entry point, so the override alone fails that build with `Could not resolve \"elliptic\"`). That replaces elliptic@6.6.1 in the lockfile with empty-pkg, removes it from the bundle, and leaves only createECDH and EC-key createSign/createVerify non-functional in the web test polyfill.",
        "expiry": "2026-11-06"
    },
    "GHSA-g3hv-75wg-m249": {
        "notes": "CVE-2026-118764: at optimizationLevel >= 2, minimatch's firstPhasePreProcess() splits every `**/..` portion of a pattern inside a fixpoint loop and secondPhasePreProcess() then compares every pair, so a short crafted pattern (14 repetitions of `**/../a/b` take about 4 s) exhausts CPU. Only the pattern triggers it, never the matched path, and only at optimizationLevel 2, which glob 9+ sets and minimatch's default (1) does not. Accepted risk: the fix exists only in minimatch 11.0.1+ (3.x, 5.x, 9.x and 10.x have no backport), no dependent in this tree except glob 14 admits 11, and 11.0 is a rewrite (no default export, makeRe and matchOne removed) that an override cannot force without breaking glob 7 and 10 matching, test-exclude 6 and eslint-plugin-import (they call minimatch as a function) and the default import in src/platform/interpreter/reservedNamedProvider.node.ts. The shipped copies do not contain the vulnerable preprocessing and are flagged by version range only: 3.1.5 (hoisted; bundled through reservedNamedProvider.node.ts's direct import, and prod-optional via @deepnote/sql-language-server -> sqlite3 -> node-gyp/cacache -> glob 7, install-time tooling) and 5.1.9 (vscode-languageclient 9.0.1). The shipped glob 14.0.3 runs its own inlined, fixed minimatch. The copies that contain the vulnerable code are dev-only and receive patterns only from this repository's own configuration: 9.0.9 under mocha 11.8.0 and rimraf 5.0.10 (via glob 10), and 10.2.5/10.2.6 under @vscode/vsce, @vscode/test-cli, c8, vscode-extension-tester and @actions/glob; in src/test/vscode-notebook-perf (mirrored in its osv-scanner.toml), 10.2.6 (hoisted) and 9.0.9 under @vscode/test-cli's mocha. The latest vscode-languageclient 11.0.0, mocha 12.0.3, @vscode/vsce 4.1.0, @vscode/test-cli 0.0.15 and test-exclude 8.0.0 still declare minimatch ^10, and eslint-plugin-import 2.32.0 declares ^3.1.2. Revisit at expiry for backports or dependents that admit minimatch >=11.0.1.",
        "expiry": "2026-11-15"
    },
    "GHSA-mrhx-c7xc-m3w5": {
        "notes": "CVE-2023-54404: zod builds one issue per invalid element while validating an oversized collection, so a huge input can exhaust memory (denial of service). Accepted risk: no patched zod 3.x release exists (range <4.6.6), and every @deepnote/* dependency pins zod to exactly 3.25.76, so a lockfile bump cannot reach a fix and an override would force an untested major under them. The shipped bundles use this zod to validate local .deepnote project files before notebook execution, including in limited-support untrusted workspaces. A crafted file that the user opens can stall or exhaust the extension host or CLI, with no data exposure. The 4.6.6 fix also only short-circuits collections with a .max()/.length()/.size() bound, so moving to zod 4 alone would clear the scanner without changing this behavior.",
        "expiry": "2026-11-10"
    },
    "GHSA-xcx6-jx8p-x3h5": {
        "notes": "CVE-2026-109461: @azure/msal-node's authorization code flow (acquireTokenByCode) may disclose request parameters to an attacker-controlled endpoint. Accepted risk: dev-only and not shipped (`npm ls @azure/msal-node --omit=dev` is empty; node_modules/** is excluded from the VSIX). One copy, @azure/msal-node@6.0.1, reached via @vscode/vsce (root and under vscode-extension-tester) -> @azure/identity@4.14.0. vsce uses @azure/identity only for `vsce publish` / `verify-pat --azure-credential`, through a chain of environment, Azure CLI and managed-identity credentials that never runs the authorization code flow; this repository only runs `vsce package`. The fix exists only on the 7.x line (first patched 7.1.0; 6.x has no backport), and the latest @azure/identity (4.14.0) still declares @azure/msal-node ^6.0.0, so a lockfile bump cannot reach it. Remove this entry and its osv-scanner.toml mirror once an @azure/identity release admits msal-node >=7.1.0.",
        "expiry": "2026-11-10"
    }
}
